##! # Error Code Documentation
##!
##! Provides detailed explanations for Quartz compiler error codes.
##! Used by `quartz --explain QZ1205` to print human-readable error docs.

## Explain error code QZ0101: Syntax error.
def explain_QZ0101(): String = "## QZ0101: Syntax Error\n\nThe parser encountered unexpected input that does not form valid Quartz syntax.\n\nCommon causes:\n- Missing 'end' keyword to close a block (def, if, match, while)\n- Unexpected token (stray punctuation, misplaced operator)\n- Unclosed string literal or parenthesis\n\nExample:\n\n  def greet(): Void\n    puts(\"hello\")\n  # missing 'end' here\n\nFix: Check that every 'def', 'if', 'match', 'while', and 'for' has\na matching 'end'. Look at the line number in the error — the actual\nmistake is usually on or just before that line.\n"

## Explain error code QZ0102: Brace-delimited struct body.
def explain_QZ0102(): String = "## QZ0102: Brace-Delimited Struct Body Not Supported\n\nStruct *definitions* in Quartz are block-delimited: each field goes on its\nown line and the struct is closed with 'end'. Curly braces '{ }' are reserved\nfor struct *literals* (creating an instance), not for declaring the struct.\n\nWrong:\n\n  struct Point { x: Int, y: Int }   # error: QZ0102\n\nRight:\n\n  struct Point\n    x: Int\n    y: Int\n  end\n\n  p = Point { x: 10, y: 20 }        # '{ }' is for the literal, here\n\nThis applies to generic structs too:\n\n  struct Box<T>\n    inner: T\n  end\n"

## Explain error code QZ0103: Misplaced binary operator (line continuation).
def explain_QZ0103(): String = "## QZ0103: Binary Operator Cannot Start an Expression\n\nA binary operator (`^`, `+`, `*`, `==`, `and`, ...) appeared where an\nexpression was expected — almost always a multi-line expression written with\nthe operator at the START of a continuation line. Quartz ends a statement at\nthe newline, so the operator becomes a stray line of its own.\n\nWrong (operator leads the continuation line):\n\n  var c = lane(0)\n          ^ lane(1)      # error: QZ0103\n          ^ lane(2)\n\nRight (operator TRAILS the previous line — the newline after a binary\noperator is a continuation):\n\n  var c = lane(0) ^\n          lane(1) ^\n          lane(2)\n\nOr keep it on one line:\n\n  var c = lane(0) ^ lane(1) ^ lane(2)\n"

## Explain error code QZ0106: Cannot assign to immutable variable.
def explain_QZ0106(): String = "## QZ0106: Cannot Assign to Immutable Variable\n\nIn Quartz, bindings are immutable by default. You cannot reassign a value\nto a binding unless it was declared with 'var'.\n\nExample:\n\n  x = 5\n  x = 10  # error: Cannot assign to immutable variable: x\n\nFix: Declare the binding with 'var' to make it mutable:\n\n  var x = 5\n  x = 10  # OK\n"

## Explain error code QZ0107: Non-exhaustive match.
def explain_QZ0107(): String = "## QZ0107: Non-Exhaustive Match\n\nA match expression does not cover all possible values of the matched type.\nFor enums, every variant must be handled. For Int and String, a wildcard\n'_' arm is required since they have infinitely many values.\n\nExample (enum):\n\n  enum Color\n    Red\n    Green\n    Blue\n  end\n\n  match color\n    Color::Red => 1\n    Color::Green => 2\n    # error: missing variant 'Blue'\n  end\n\nExample (Int):\n\n  match x\n    0 => \"zero\"\n    1 => \"one\"\n    # error: Int requires wildcard '_' pattern\n  end\n\nFix: Add the missing variant, or add a wildcard '_' arm as a catch-all:\n\n  match color\n    Color::Red => 1\n    _ => 0\n  end\n"

## Explain error code QZ0150: Impossible intersection type.
def explain_QZ0150(): String = "## QZ0150: Impossible Intersection Type\n\nAn intersection type 'A & B' requires a value to satisfy all member types\nsimultaneously. When the member types are incompatible primitives (like\nInt & String), no value can ever satisfy the constraint.\n\nExample:\n\n  def foo(x: Int & String): Int  # error: impossible intersection\n    return 0\n  end\n\nFix: Use a union type (Int | String) if you want to accept either type,\nor remove the conflicting member from the intersection.\n"

## Explain error code QZ0151: Intersection field conflict.
def explain_QZ0151(): String = "## QZ0151: Record Intersection Field Conflict\n\nTwo record types in an intersection have the same field name but different\ntypes. When intersecting records, shared fields must have identical types.\n\nExample:\n\n  def foo(r: { x: Int } & { x: String }): Int  # error: conflicting field 'x'\n    return 0\n  end\n\nFix: Ensure shared field names have the same type in all intersected records.\n"

## Explain error code QZ0160: Unknown trait in impl return.
def explain_QZ0160(): String = "## QZ0160: Unknown Trait in impl Return Type\n\nA function declares 'impl TraitName' as its return type, but the trait\nhas not been defined.\n\nExample:\n\n  def make(): impl Printable  # error: unknown trait 'Printable'\n    return 42\n  end\n\nFix: Define the trait, or check the spelling:\n\n  trait Printable\n    def show(self): String\n    end\n  end\n"

## Explain error code QZ0161: impl return type conflict.
def explain_QZ0161(): String = "## QZ0161: impl Return Type Conflict\n\nA function with 'impl Trait' return type returns different concrete types\nfrom different code paths. All return paths must yield the same concrete type.\n\nExample:\n\n  def make(flag: Bool): impl Showable\n    if flag\n      return Foo { x: 1 }\n    end\n    return Bar { y: 2 }  # error: conflict — Foo vs Bar\n  end\n\nFix: Return the same concrete type from all branches.\n"

## Explain error code QZ0162: Concrete type does not implement trait.
def explain_QZ0162(): String = "## QZ0162: Type Does Not Implement Trait\n\nA function declares 'impl Trait' as its return type, but the concrete type\nbeing returned does not actually implement that trait.\n\nExample:\n\n  trait Showable\n    def show(self): String\n    end\n  end\n\n  struct Plain\n    x: Int\n  end\n\n  def make(): impl Showable\n    return Plain { x: 1 }  # error: Plain does not implement Showable\n  end\n\nFix: Add an extend block for the trait:\n\n  extend Plain with Showable\n    def show(self): String = \"Plain\"\n  end\n"

## Explain error code QZ0163: Recursive impl Trait inference.
def explain_QZ0163(): String = "## QZ0163: Recursive impl Trait Inference\n\nA function declares 'impl Trait' as its return type and calls itself\nrecursively in its body. The compiler infers the opaque return type from\nthe body's concrete return values, but a self-call would make that\ninference depend on its own result — the inference diverges.\n\nThis is the same restriction Rust enforced before `type_alias_impl_trait`.\n\nExample:\n\n  def make_iter(n: Int): impl Iterator<Int>\n    if n <= 0\n      return empty_iter()\n    end\n    return make_iter(n - 1)  # error: QZ0163 — recursive impl Trait\n  end\n\nFix: Name the concrete return type explicitly. If both branches return\nthe same concrete type T, change the return annotation to T. If they\nreturn different types, hoist the recursion behind an enum or a\ntype-erased wrapper.\n\n  def make_iter(n: Int): EmptyIter\n    return empty_iter()\n  end\n"

## Explain error code QZ0165: Concrete type for impl Trait cannot be inferred.
def explain_QZ0165(): String = "## QZ0165: Cannot Infer Concrete Type for impl Trait Return\n\nA function declares 'impl Trait' as its return type, but its body has\nno return statement the compiler can read a concrete type from. This\nhappens when the body only `panic`s, loops forever, or otherwise never\nproduces a typed return value.\n\nWithout a concrete type, the compiler cannot:\n- verify the type implements the declared trait (QZ0162)\n- monomorphize the function for downstream callers\n- emit code for the opaque return\n\nExample:\n\n  def todo(): impl Iterator<Int> = panic(\"not yet\")\n  # error: QZ0165 — body has no inferable return\n\nFix: Replace `impl Trait` with a named placeholder type that does\nimplement the trait, or write a body that returns a concrete value:\n\n  def todo(): EmptyIter = empty_iter()\n\nFor truly-unimplemented stubs, return a minimal real value:\n\n  def todo(): impl Iterator<Int>\n    return empty_iter()\n  end\n"

## Explain error code QZ0166: Reserved — dyn Trait incompatibility (forward-compat).
def explain_QZ0166(): String = "## QZ0166: dyn Trait Incompatibility (Reserved)\n\nThis error code is reserved for a future Quartz feature: dynamic dispatch\nthrough trait objects (`dyn Trait`). When that feature lands, traits that\nhave methods returning `impl Trait` will be incompatible with `dyn Trait`\nbecause the return type would not be object-safe.\n\nQuartz does not currently support `dyn Trait`, so this error is never\nemitted. The code is reserved so the eventual feature has a stable\nnumber to attach to.\n\nIf you see this error in the wild, please file a bug — the type checker\nshould not be emitting it yet.\n"

## Explain error code QZ0167: Reserved — opaque type cannot be named explicitly.
def explain_QZ0167(): String = "## QZ0167: Opaque Type Cannot Be Named Explicitly (Reserved)\n\nThis error code is reserved for the impl Trait opacity work (sprint Phase 7).\nWhen opacity lands, callers will not be able to write the underlying\nconcrete type in an annotation. For example:\n\n  def iter(v: Vec<Int>): impl Iterator<Int> = vec_iter_new(v)\n\n  var x: VecIter = iter(v)  # would be QZ0167\n\nThe fix is to drop the annotation and let inference do its job:\n\n  var x = iter(v)\n\nQuartz does not yet enforce opacity, so this error is reserved but not\nemitted. The code is held so the eventual feature has a stable number.\n"

## Explain error code QZ0168: Reserved — incompatible opaque types.
def explain_QZ0168(): String = "## QZ0168: Incompatible Opaque Types (Reserved)\n\nThis error code is reserved for the impl Trait opacity work (sprint Phase 7).\nTwo functions returning `impl Trait` produce distinct opaque identities\neven when their underlying concrete types match, so they cannot be\nunified at a join point:\n\n  def make_a(): impl Iterator<Int> = vec_iter_new(va)\n  def make_b(): impl Iterator<Int> = vec_iter_new(vb)\n\n  var x = if cond then make_a() else make_b() end  # would be QZ0168\n\nThe fix is to extract a shared type alias or hoist the shared concrete\ntype:\n\n  var x: VecIter = if cond then vec_iter_new(va) else vec_iter_new(vb) end\n\nQuartz does not yet enforce opacity, so this error is reserved but not\nemitted. The code is held so the eventual feature has a stable number.\n"

## Explain error code QZ0200: Return type mismatch.
def explain_QZ0200(): String = "## QZ0200: Return Type Mismatch\n\nThe value being returned does not match the function's declared return type.\n\nExample:\n\n  def get_count(): Int\n    return \"none\"  # error: expected Int, got String\n  end\n\nFix: Either change the return value to match the declared type, or\nchange the function's return type annotation.\n"

## Explain error code QZ0201: Type mismatch.
def explain_QZ0201(): String = "## QZ0201: Type Mismatch\n\nThe compiler found a value of one type where a different type was expected.\n\nCommon causes:\n- Returning a String from a function declared to return Int\n- Passing wrong argument type to a function\n- Assigning a value of incompatible type to a variable\n\nExample:\n\n  def add(x: Int, y: Int): Int\n    return x + y\n  end\n\n  add(1, \"two\")  # error: expected Int, got String\n\nFix: Ensure the types match. Convert values if needed.\n"

## Explain error code QZ0202: Match arm type mismatch.
def explain_QZ0202(): String = "## QZ0202: Match Arm Type Mismatch\n\nThe arms of a match expression return different types. All arms must\nreturn the same type since the match is a single expression.\n\nExample:\n\n  result = match x\n    1 => 42        # Int\n    _ => \"hello\"   # String — error: mismatch\n  end\n\nFix: Make all arms return the same type:\n\n  result = match x\n    1 => 42\n    _ => 0\n  end\n"

## Explain error code QZ0203: Type inference conflict.
def explain_QZ0203(): String = "## QZ0203: Type Inference Conflict\n\nThe compiler inferred the element type of a container from its first\nconstraining use, but a later use pushes a value of a different type.\n\nCommon causes:\n- Pushing both Int and String into the same Vec\n- Using vec_set with a different type than what was pushed earlier\n\nExample:\n\n  var items = vec_new()\n  items.push(42)       # element type inferred as Int\n  items.push(\"hello\")  # error: element type is Int but got String\n\nFix: Use separate containers for different types, or add an explicit\ntype annotation:\n\n  var items = vec_new<Int>()\n"

## Explain error code QZ0204: Ambiguous type inference.
def explain_QZ0204(): String = "## QZ0204: Ambiguous Type Inference\n\nA container was created but its element type was never constrained.\nThe compiler could not determine what type the container holds.\n\nCommon causes:\n- Creating a Vec with vec_new() but never pushing values into it\n- Only calling .size on a container without adding elements\n\nExample:\n\n  var items = vec_new()\n  puts(items.size)  # element type never constrained\n\nFix: Add an explicit type annotation:\n\n  var items = vec_new<Int>()\n"

## Explain error code QZ0301: Unknown struct.
def explain_QZ0301(): String = "## QZ0301: Unknown Struct\n\nThe compiler encountered a struct name that has not been defined.\n\nCommon causes:\n- Typo in the struct name\n- Forgot to import the module that defines the struct\n- The struct is defined after it is used (Quartz requires declaration before use)\n\nExample:\n\n  p = Pount { x: 1, y: 2 }  # error: Unknown struct: Pount\n\nFix: Check the spelling. If the struct is in another module, add an import.\n"

## Explain error code QZ0302: Unknown enum.
def explain_QZ0302(): String = "## QZ0302: Unknown Enum\n\nThe compiler encountered an enum name that has not been defined.\n\nCommon causes:\n- Typo in the enum name\n- Forgot to import the module that defines the enum\n\nFix: Check the spelling and imports.\n"

## Explain error code QZ0303: Unknown field.
def explain_QZ0303(): String = "## QZ0303: Unknown Field\n\nA field access refers to a field that does not exist on the struct.\n\nExample:\n\n  struct Point\n    x: Int\n    y: Int\n  end\n\n  p = Point { x: 1, y: 2 }\n  puts(p.z)  # error: unknown field 'z' on Point\n\nFix: Check the field name spelling. Use the struct definition to see\nwhich fields are available.\n"

## Explain error code QZ0401: Undefined function.
def explain_QZ0401(): String = "## QZ0401: Undefined Function\n\nThe compiler could not find a function with the given name.\n\nCommon causes:\n- Typo in the function name\n- The function is in another module but not imported\n- Using UFCS syntax but the function is not in scope\n\nExample:\n\n  prntln(\"hello\")  # error: Undefined function: prntln\n\nFix: Check spelling. For module functions, use module::function() or import.\n"

## Explain error code QZ0406: Option/Result method on wrong receiver.
def explain_QZ0406(): String = "## QZ0406: Option/Result Method On Wrong Receiver\n\nThe methods `unwrap`, `unwrap_or`, `unwrap_err`, `expect`, `is_some`,\n`is_none`, `is_ok`, and `is_err` are only valid on `Option<T>` or\n`Result<T, E>`. Calling one on any other receiver used to be silently\naccepted and miscompiled: the receiver's first word was read as an Option\nniche / boxed payload, producing garbage (a bogus \"unwrap on None\") or a\nSIGSEGV (e.g. a `Vec<Int>` element read as a pointer).\n\nExample:\n\n  x = 5\n  y = x.unwrap()   # error: QZ0406 — Int is not Option or Result\n\n  v = [1, 2, 3]\n  b = v.is_some()  # error: QZ0406 — Vec is not Option or Result\n\nFix:\n- Only call these on a value whose type is `Option<T>` or `Result<T, E>`.\n- If you meant to test presence of a plain value, just use it directly.\n- If you defined your own method of the same name, ensure it is in scope\n  as an impl/extend/UFCS method on the receiver's type (it takes priority).\n"

## Explain error code QZ0407: Mutating method without `&mut self` receiver.
def explain_QZ0407(): String = "## QZ0407: Method Mutates `self` Without `&mut self`\n\nA method whose body directly assigns a field of `self` (via `self.field = …`\nor the `@field = …` shorthand) must declare a `&mut self` receiver. Under the\nvalue-default semantics, a bare-`self` (or `&self`) receiver is passed by copy,\nso the mutation would be lost on the caller's value. `&mut self` is the explicit\nmutable-borrow receiver — the callee mutates the caller's slot in place.\n\nOnly DIRECT field mutation is enforced: a method that merely forwards to another\nself-method (e.g. `return self.peek()`) keeps its bare `self` receiver.\n\nExample:\n\n  struct Counter\n    n: Int\n  end\n\n  def Counter.bump(self): Void\n    self.n = self.n + 1   # error: QZ0407 — mutates self without &mut self\n  end\n\nFix: change the receiver `self` to `&mut self`:\n\n  def Counter.bump(&mut self): Void\n    self.n = self.n + 1   # ok\n  end\n"

## Explain error code QZ0408: overlapping mutable access (static exclusivity).
def explain_QZ0408(): String = "## QZ0408: Overlapping Access (Law of Exclusivity)\n\nWhile a `&mut p` borrow is live, no other live access to overlapping storage is\nallowed. Two arguments of a single call are simultaneously live, so passing the\nsame storage as a `&mut` alongside any other access to an overlapping path — a\nsecond `&mut`, or a plain read/write — is a conflict.\n\nUnder value-default semantics a `&mut` mutates the caller's slot in place; a\nsecond overlapping access could observe a half-updated value or race the write.\n\nExample:\n\n  swap(&mut x, &mut x)   # warning: QZ0408 — both borrow `x`\n  f(&mut x, x)           # warning: QZ0408 — &mut and a read of `x` overlap\n\nFix: restructure so the accesses don't overlap in time — take one borrow, finish\nwith it, then take the other. Distinct fields (`&mut p.a`, `&p.b`) and provably-\ndistinct constant indices (`&mut a[0]`, `&mut a[1]`) do NOT overlap.\n"

## Explain error code QZ0409: escaping second-class borrow (non-escape rule).
def explain_QZ0409(): String = "## QZ0409: Escaping Second-Class Borrow\n\nA `&x` / `&mut x` borrow is a second-class reference: it is valid only for the\nduration of the call it is passed to. It must not outlive the storage it points\nat, so it may not (a) be stored into a field or array element, (b) be returned,\nor (c) be captured by an escaping closure (a lambda passed to `go`/`spawn`, a\nstored closure, or a non-whitelisted callee).\n\nQuartz has no lifetimes; this structural rule is what keeps a borrow from\ndangling — the same soundness condition the mutable capture-by-reference\nlowering relies on.\n\nExample:\n\n  self.r = &mut x        # warning: QZ0409 — stored into a field\n  return &mut x          # warning: QZ0409 — returned\n  go { &mut x }          # warning: QZ0409 — captured by an escaping closure\n\nFix: keep the borrow call-scoped (pass it directly as an argument and let it die\nat the call), or pass by value (a copy) where a value suffices — cheap under the\nvalue-default semantics.\n"

## Explain error code QZ0410: dynamic exclusivity violation (runtime trap).
def explain_QZ0410(): String = "## QZ0410: Dynamic Exclusivity Violation (runtime trap)\n\nThis is not a compile-time error — it is a runtime trap raised by\n`qz_exclusivity_panic` under `--exclusivity-check`. It fires when two accesses\nto overlapping storage are live at the same time and at least one of them is a\nmodification. The static exclusivity pass (QZ0408) could not prove exclusivity\nhere — typically because the access goes through a dynamic index or a\nheap-reachable path — so the check was deferred to runtime.\n\nAt runtime each non-instantaneous access (a `&mut` borrow, a mutating method\ncall) brackets its live range with `qz_begin_access` / `qz_end_access`, which\npush/pop a record on a per-thread access list. When a `begin` finds an existing\nlive access to the same base object where one side is a modify, it traps with a\ntwo-location trace (the earlier access and the current one).\n\nExample (dynamic index into an aliasable buffer):\n\n  modify_both(&mut a[i], &mut a[j])   # traps if i == j at runtime\n\nFix: same as QZ0408 — restructure so the accesses don't overlap in time. Finish\none borrow before taking the other, or copy by value where a value suffices.\nBuild WITHOUT `--exclusivity-check` to disable the runtime instrumentation (the\ncheck defaults off; IR is byte-identical when the flag is not set).\n"

## Explain error code QZ0405: Ambiguous wildcard import.
def explain_QZ0405(): String = "## QZ0405: Ambiguous Call From Wildcard Imports\n\nTwo or more modules imported with `import * from` define a function with\nthe same name. A bare call to that name is ambiguous — the compiler picks\nthe first-imported one and warns, but that may not be what you meant.\n\nThis is most dangerous for private helpers: if `sha256.qz` and `sha512.qz`\nboth define a private `_compress`, importing both via `*` can silently wire\none module's internal calls to the other's helper.\n\nExample:\n\n  import * from sha256   # defines _compress (u32)\n  import * from sha384   # transitively defines _compress (u64)\n\n  sha256(\"\")             # warning: ambiguous '_compress'\n\nFix (any of):\n- Import qualified: `import sha256`, then call `sha256$digest(...)`.\n- Rename on import: `from sha256 import digest as sha256_digest`.\n- In the stdlib, module-prefix private helpers (`_sha256_compress`).\n"

## Explain error code QZ0403: Undefined variable.
def explain_QZ0403(): String = "## QZ0403: Undefined Variable\n\nThe compiler could not find a variable with the given name in the current scope.\n\nCommon causes:\n- Typo in the variable name\n- The variable was defined in a different scope (e.g., inside an if block)\n- Using the variable before it was defined\n\nExample:\n\n  x = 10\n  puts(y)  # error: Undefined variable: y\n\nFix: Check spelling and ensure the variable is in scope.\n"

## Explain error code QZ0501: Unquote outside of quote.
def explain_QZ0501(): String = "## QZ0501: Unquote Outside of Quote Block\n\nThe 'unquote()' form can only appear inside a 'quote ... end' block\nwithin a macro definition. Using it outside a quote is meaningless.\n\nExample:\n\n  macro bad(x)\n    unquote(x)  # error: unquote outside of quote block\n  end\n\nFix: Wrap the unquote in a quote block:\n\n  macro good(x)\n    quote\n      unquote(x) + 1\n    end\n  end\n"

## Explain error code QZ0502: Macro expansion depth exceeded.
def explain_QZ0502(): String = "## QZ0502: Macro Expansion Depth Exceeded\n\nA macro expanded too many times, likely due to infinite recursion.\nThe compiler limits expansion depth to prevent hangs.\n\nExample:\n\n  macro loop(x)\n    quote\n      $loop(unquote(x))  # expands forever\n    end\n  end\n\nFix: Ensure your macro has a base case that does not recursively invoke\nitself, or restructure to avoid recursive macro expansion.\n"

## Explain error code QZ0503: Unknown macro.
def explain_QZ0503(): String = "## QZ0503: Unknown Macro\n\nThe compiler encountered a macro call '$name(...)' where 'name' is not\na recognized built-in or user-defined macro.\n\nBuilt-in macros: $unwrap, $assert, $debug\n\nUser-defined macros must be declared with 'macro' before use:\n\n  macro double(x)\n    quote\n      unquote(x) * 2\n    end\n  end\n\n  $double(21)  # expands to: 21 * 2\n\nFix: Check the macro name spelling. If it's a user-defined macro,\nensure the 'macro' definition appears before the call site.\n"

## Explain error code QZ0504: Macro expects ident argument.
def explain_QZ0504(): String = "## QZ0504: Macro Expects Identifier Argument\n\nA macro parameter was declared with the ':ident' fragment specifier,\nbut the argument passed is not a simple identifier.\n\nExample:\n\n  macro make(name:ident)\n    quote\n      var unquote(name) = 0\n    end\n  end\n\n  $make(1 + 2)  # error: expects ident, got expression\n\nFix: Pass a bare identifier (variable or function name), not an expression.\n"

## Explain error code QZ0505: Macro expects block argument.
def explain_QZ0505(): String = "## QZ0505: Macro Expects Block Argument\n\nA macro parameter was declared with the ':block' fragment specifier,\nbut the argument passed is not a block (do ... end).\n\nExample:\n\n  macro with_block(body:block)\n    quote\n      unquote(body)\n    end\n  end\n\n  $with_block(42)  # error: expects block, got expression\n\nFix: Pass a do ... end block as the argument.\n"

## Explain error code QZ0506: Unknown unquote_each separator.
def explain_QZ0506(): String = "## QZ0506: Unknown unquote_each Separator\n\nThe unquote_each form was given an unrecognized separator token.\nSupported separators are '+' for combining spliced elements.\n\nFix: Use a supported separator, or omit the separator argument.\n"

## Explain error code QZ0507: Unquote references unknown parameter.
def explain_QZ0507(): String = "## QZ0507: Unquote References Unknown Parameter\n\nAn 'unquote(name)' inside a quote block refers to a parameter that\ndoes not exist in the enclosing macro definition.\n\nExample:\n\n  macro add_one(x)\n    quote\n      unquote(y) + 1  # error: unknown parameter 'y'\n    end\n  end\n\nFix: Check the parameter name spelling. It must match one of the\nmacro's declared parameters.\n"

## Explain error code QZ0508: Failed to re-parse macro expansion.
def explain_QZ0508(): String = "## QZ0508: Failed to Re-Parse Macro Expansion\n\nAfter expanding a macro, the resulting code could not be parsed as valid\nQuartz syntax. This usually means the macro's quote block generates\nmalformed code when arguments are substituted in.\n\nFix: Check the macro's quote block. Mentally substitute the arguments\nand verify the expanded code is valid Quartz syntax. Common issues:\n- Missing operators between unquote'd expressions\n- Unbalanced parentheses after expansion\n- Keywords in unexpected positions\n"

## QZ0509 was the `$try` wrong-argument-count diagnostic. Retired in
## Operation Piezoelectric Effects, Phase 1, Milestone A when the `$try`
## macro was replaced by the `try` prefix keyword. The code number is
## kept dead so downstream tooling catches stale references.

## Explain error code QZ0510: $unwrap wrong argument count.
def explain_QZ0510(): String = "## QZ0510: $unwrap Expects Exactly 1 Argument\n\nThe $unwrap macro takes exactly one argument — an Option value. It\nextracts the Some value or panics if the value is None.\n\nExample:\n\n  var result = $unwrap(find_item(id))\n\nFix: Pass exactly one expression to $unwrap.\n"

## Explain error code QZ0511: $assert wrong argument count.
def explain_QZ0511(): String = "## QZ0511: $assert Expects At Least 1 Argument\n\nThe $assert macro requires at least one argument — a boolean condition.\nAn optional second argument provides a custom error message.\n\nExample:\n\n  $assert(x > 0)\n  $assert(x > 0, \"x must be positive\")\n\nFix: Pass a boolean expression as the first argument.\n"

## Explain error code QZ0512: $debug wrong argument count.
def explain_QZ0512(): String = "## QZ0512: $debug Expects Exactly 1 Argument\n\nThe $debug macro takes exactly one argument — an expression to inspect.\nIt prints the expression text and its value at runtime.\n\nExample:\n\n  $debug(x + 1)  # prints: [debug] x + 1 = 6\n\nFix: Pass exactly one expression to $debug.\n"

## Explain error code QZ0520: Failed to re-parse @derive expansion.
def explain_QZ0520(): String = "## QZ0520: Failed to Re-Parse @derive Expansion\n\nThe code generated by an @derive annotation could not be parsed. This\nis typically an internal compiler issue — the derive system produced\ninvalid Quartz code.\n\nValid derive traits: Eq, Hash, Show, Clone, Ord\n\nExample:\n\n  @derive(Eq, Show)\n  struct Point\n    x: Int\n    y: Int\n  end\n\nFix: Ensure you're using supported derive traits. If the error persists\nwith a valid derive, this may be a compiler bug — please report it.\n"

## Explain error code QZ0521: Unknown derive trait.
def explain_QZ0521(): String = "## QZ0521: Unknown Derive Trait\n\nThe @derive annotation references a trait that cannot be auto-derived.\n\nValid derive traits: Eq, Hash, Show, Clone, Ord\n\nExample:\n\n  @derive(Eq, Hash, Show)\n  struct Point\n    x: Int\n    y: Int\n  end\n\nFix: Use only the supported derive traits listed above.\n"

## Explain error code QZ0530: Reserved keyword used as identifier.
def explain_QZ0530(): String = "## QZ0530: Reserved Keyword Cannot Be Used as Identifier\n\nYou used a reserved keyword (like `end`, `def`, `if`, `for`) where an\nidentifier is expected — typically at a `var` binding, function\nparameter, for-loop variable, or match pattern binding.\n\nExample of the error:\n\n  var end = 42              # error: 'end' is reserved\n  def f(if: Int): Int       # error: 'if' is reserved\n  for match in 0..3 ... end # error: 'match' is reserved\n\nFix: Pick a name that is not a reserved keyword. Names that happen to\nstart with a keyword are fine — e.g. `endpoint`, `match_count`,\n`if_val` — as long as the full identifier is not itself the keyword.\n\nControl case (compiles fine):\n\n  var endpoint = 42         # OK — 'endpoint' is not reserved\n"

## Explain error code QZ0550: Circular dependency.
def explain_QZ0550(): String = "## QZ0550: Circular Dependency Detected\n\nTwo or more modules import each other in a cycle. Quartz does not\nsupport circular imports — the dependency graph must be acyclic.\n\nExample:\n\n  # a.qz\n  import b\n\n  # b.qz\n  import a  # error: circular dependency\n\nFix: Break the cycle by extracting shared definitions into a third\nmodule that both can import, or restructure the code so one module\ndoes not need to import the other.\n"

## Explain error code QZ0551: Module not found.
def explain_QZ0551(): String = "## QZ0551: Module Not Found\n\nThe compiler could not find a module file for the given import.\n\nSearch order for 'import foo':\n  1. base_path/foo.qz\n  2. base_path/foo/mod.qz\n  3. base_path/frontend/foo.qz\n  4. base_path/backend/foo.qz\n  5. base_path/middle/foo.qz\n  6. -I include paths\n\nCommon causes:\n- Typo in the module name\n- Missing -I include path flag\n- File not yet created\n\nFix: Check spelling, verify the file exists, or add -I flags.\n"

## Explain error code QZ0552: Unknown C header.
def explain_QZ0552(): String = "## QZ0552: Unknown C Header\n\nA 'cimport' statement references a C header that the compiler does not\nrecognize. The compiler has a built-in list of known C headers for FFI.\n\nExample:\n\n  cimport \"nonexistent.h\"  # error: unknown C header\n\nFix: Check the header name spelling. For custom C libraries, use\n'extern' function declarations instead of cimport.\n"

## Explain error code QZ0600: Not a compile-time constant.
def explain_QZ0600(): String = "## QZ0600: Not a Compile-Time Constant\n\nA const expression references a name that is not available at compile time.\nOnly literals, other const values, and const functions can be used.\n\nExample:\n\n  var x = 5\n  const Y = x  # error: 'x' is not a compile-time constant\n\nFix: Use a literal or another const value:\n\n  const X = 5\n  const Y = X + 1  # OK\n"

## Explain error code QZ0601: Non-const function in const context.
def explain_QZ0601(): String = "## QZ0601: Non-Const Function in Const Context\n\nA const expression calls a function that is not marked as a const\nfunction. Only functions that can be evaluated at compile time are\nallowed in const contexts.\n\nExample:\n\n  def compute(x: Int): Int = x * 2\n\n  const VAL = compute(21)  # error: 'compute' is not a const function\n\nFix: Either use a literal value, or mark the function as const if it\nonly uses compile-time-safe operations.\n"

## Explain error code QZ0602: Const-eval iteration limit exceeded.
def explain_QZ0602(): String = "## QZ0602: Const-Eval Iteration Limit Exceeded\n\nA const expression required more than 1,000,000 evaluation steps.\nThis usually means an infinite loop in a const function.\n\nExample:\n\n  const def loop(x: Int): Int = loop(x)  # infinite recursion\n  const VAL = loop(0)  # error: iteration limit exceeded\n\nFix: Ensure const functions terminate. Check for infinite recursion\nor unbounded loops.\n"

## Explain error code QZ0603: Cannot resolve struct for field access.
def explain_QZ0603(): String = "## QZ0603: Cannot Resolve Struct Type for Field Access\n\nThe compiler could not determine which struct type a field access\noperates on. This prevents calculating the correct field offset.\n\nThis typically happens when type information is lost during MIR\nlowering — the expression's type was erased or not propagated.\n\nFix: Add an explicit type annotation to the variable being accessed,\nor restructure the code so the compiler can infer the struct type.\n"

## Explain error code QZ1201: Linear type not consumed.
def explain_QZ1201(): String = "## QZ1201: Linear Type Not Consumed\n\nA linear struct must be consumed exactly once before it goes out of scope.\nConsuming means passing it to a function that takes ownership.\n\nExample:\n\n  linear struct Handle\n    fd: Int\n  end\n\n  def main(): Int\n    h = Handle { fd: 42 }\n    return 0  # error: linear type Handle not consumed\n  end\n\nFix: Call a consuming function (e.g., close(h)) before the scope ends.\n"

## Explain error code QZ1202: Linear value consumed twice.
def explain_QZ1202(): String = "## QZ1202: Linear Value Consumed Twice\n\nA linear value was passed to two different consuming functions. Linear\ntypes must be consumed exactly once — not zero times, not twice.\n\nExample:\n\n  linear struct Token\n    id: Int\n  end\n\n  def use_token(t: Token): Int = t.id\n\n  def main(): Int\n    var t = Token { id: 1 }\n    use_token(t)  # first consumption — OK\n    use_token(t)  # error: consumed twice\n    return 0\n  end\n\nFix: Remove the second consumption. If you need the value twice,\nrestructure to only pass it once.\n"

## Explain error code QZ1203: Use of consumed linear value.
def explain_QZ1203(): String = "## QZ1203: Use of Consumed Linear Value\n\nA linear value was accessed after it was already consumed by a function.\nOnce a linear value is consumed, it no longer exists.\n\nExample:\n\n  linear struct Token\n    id: Int\n  end\n\n  def eat(t: Token): Int = t.id\n\n  def main(): Int\n    var t = Token { id: 1 }\n    eat(t)       # t is consumed here\n    var x = t.id # error: t was already consumed\n    return x\n  end\n\nFix: Save any values you need from the linear type before consuming it:\n\n  var id = t.id\n  eat(t)\n  return id\n"

## Explain error code QZ1204: Borrow of consumed value.
def explain_QZ1204(): String = "## QZ1204: Cannot Borrow Consumed Value\n\nYou attempted to borrow (&) a value that has already been consumed by\na move or function call. Once a value is moved, it no longer exists\nand cannot be referenced.\n\nExample:\n\n  var a = Res { x: 1 }\n  consume(a)       # a is moved here\n  var r = &a       # error: a was already consumed\n\nFix: Borrow the value before consuming it, or restructure so you\ndon't need the borrow after the move.\n"

## Explain error code QZ1205: Conflicting exclusive borrow.
def explain_QZ1205(): String = "## QZ1205: Conflicting Exclusive Borrow\n\nYou cannot create two mutable (&mut) borrows of the same variable at the\nsame time. Mutable borrows require exclusive access.\n\nExample:\n\n  var x = 42\n  p1 = &mut x\n  p2 = &mut x  # error: conflicting exclusive borrow\n\nFix: Restructure your code so only one mutable borrow exists at a time.\nDrop the first borrow before creating the second.\n"

## Explain error code QZ1206: Conflicting borrows.
def explain_QZ1206(): String = "## QZ1206: Conflicting Borrows\n\nYou cannot have a mutable borrow (&mut) and a shared borrow (&) of the\nsame variable at the same time. Mutable borrows require exclusive access.\n\nExample:\n\n  var a = Res { x: 1 }\n  var r1 = &mut a    # exclusive borrow\n  var r2 = &a        # error: conflicts with mutable borrow\n\nFix: Use either shared borrows or a single mutable borrow, not both.\nRestructure so the mutable borrow is released before creating a shared one.\n"

## Explain error code QZ1207: Inconsistent linear consumption across branches.
def explain_QZ1207(): String = "## QZ1207: Inconsistent Linear Consumption Across Branches\n\nA linear value is consumed in some branches of a conditional but not\nothers. Linear types must be consumed exactly once on every code path.\n\nExample:\n\n  linear struct Token\n    id: Int\n  end\n\n  def eat(t: Token): Int = t.id\n\n  if condition\n    eat(token)    # consumed in then-branch\n  end\n  # error: 'token' not consumed in else-branch\n\nFix: Consume the linear value in all branches, or restructure so the\nconsumption happens unconditionally after the branch.\n"

## Explain error code QZ1208: Mutable borrow of immutable binding.
def explain_QZ1208(): String = "## QZ1208: Mutable Borrow of Immutable Binding\n\nYou cannot create a mutable borrow (&mut) of an immutable binding.\nSince the binding is not mutable, allowing &mut would violate the\nimmutability guarantee.\n\nExample:\n\n  a = Res { x: 1 }     # immutable (no 'var')\n  var r = &mut a        # error: cannot mutably borrow immutable 'a'\n\nFix: Declare the binding with 'var' to allow mutable borrows:\n\n  var a = Res { x: 1 }\n  var r = &mut a        # OK\n"

## Explain error code QZ1209: Mutation of borrowed variable.
def explain_QZ1209(): String = "## QZ1209: Mutation While Borrowed\n\nYou cannot modify a variable while it is borrowed. The borrow guarantees\nthe variable won't change while someone holds a reference to it.\n\nWith Non-Lexical Lifetimes (NLL), borrows expire at their last use, not\nat the end of their scope. If the borrow is not used after the mutation,\nthe compiler will allow it.\n\nExample (error):\n\n  var x = 42\n  var r = &x\n  x = 99      # error: r is still live (used on next line)\n  var y = r   # last use of r\n\nExample (OK with NLL):\n\n  var x = 42\n  var r = &x\n  var y = r   # last use of r — borrow released here\n  x = 99      # OK: r's borrow has expired\n\nFix: Move the last use of the borrow before the mutation.\n"

## Explain error code QZ1210: Return borrow of local.
def explain_QZ1210(): String = "## QZ1210: Return Borrow of Local Variable\n\nYou cannot return a borrow (&) of a local variable. The variable will be\ndestroyed when the function returns, leaving a dangling reference.\n\nExample:\n\n  def bad(): &Int\n    var x = 42\n    return &x  # error: cannot return borrow of local variable\n  end\n\nFix: Return the value directly, or allocate on the heap.\n"

## Explain error code QZ1211: Store borrow in struct field.
def explain_QZ1211(): String = "## QZ1211: Store Borrow in Struct Field\n\nYou cannot store a borrow (&) in a struct field. The struct may outlive\nthe borrowed variable, creating a dangling reference.\n\nExample:\n\n  struct Holder\n    ptr: Int\n  end\n\n  var x = 42\n  h = Holder { ptr: &x }  # error: cannot store borrow in struct field\n\nFix: Store the value directly, or use a different ownership pattern.\n"

## Explain error code QZ1212: Use of moved value.
def explain_QZ1212(): String = "## QZ1212: Use of Moved Value\n\nA value was used after it was moved to another binding or function.\nTypes that implement Drop are moved (not copied) when assigned or passed\nby value. After a move, the source binding is dead.\n\nExample:\n\n  struct Connection\n    fd: Int\n  end\n  extend Connection with Drop\n    def drop(self): Void = close(self.fd)\n  end\n\n  var c = Connection { fd: 42 }\n  var c2 = c       # c is moved to c2\n  use(c)           # error: use of moved value 'c'\n\nFix: Use a borrow (&c) if you don't need ownership, or restructure\nto avoid using the value after it has been moved.\n"

## Explain error code QZ1214: Inconsistent move across branches.
def explain_QZ1214(): String = "## QZ1214: Inconsistent Move Across Branches\n\nA moveable value is consumed in one branch of an if/else but not the\nother. After the if/else, the compiler cannot know whether the value\nis still live.\n\nExample:\n\n  var c = Connection { fd: 42 }\n  if condition\n    consume(c)     # c moved in then-branch\n  end\n  use(c)           # error: c may have been moved\n\nFix: Either consume the value in both branches, or neither.\n"

## Explain error code QZ1215: Cannot implement both Copy and Drop.
def explain_QZ1215(): String = "## QZ1215: Cannot Implement Both Copy and Drop\n\nA type cannot implement both Copy and Drop. Copy means the value is\nfreely duplicated (no ownership tracking). Drop means the value has\ncleanup logic that must run exactly once. These are contradictory.\n\nExample:\n\n  struct Handle\n    fd: Int\n  end\n  extend Handle with Drop\n    def drop(self): Void = close(self.fd)\n  end\n  extend Handle with Copy  # error: cannot implement both Copy and Drop\n\nFix: Choose one. If cleanup is needed, use Drop (and accept move\nsemantics). If free copying is needed, use Copy (and don't implement\nDrop).\n"

## Explain error code QZ1216: Partial move.
def explain_QZ1216(): String = "## QZ1216: Use of Partially Moved Value\n\nA field of a struct was moved out (passed to a consuming function),\nand then the same field or the whole struct was accessed again.\nOnce a field is moved out, it and the containing struct are invalid.\n\nExample:\n\n  var outer = Outer { a: inner, b: 2 }\n  consume(outer.a)   # outer.a is moved\n  var x = outer.a    # error: use of moved field 'outer.a'\n\nFix: Save the field value before moving, or restructure to avoid\naccessing the struct after a partial move.\n"

## Explain error code QZ1218: Dangling borrow (lifetime).
def explain_QZ1218(): String = "## QZ1218: Dangling Borrow (Lifetime Violation)\n\nA borrow would outlive the variable it references. This happens when a\nvariable in an inner scope is borrowed by a binding in an outer scope,\nor when a borrow survives past the scope exit of its source.\n\nExample:\n\n  var r = 0\n  if true\n    var inner = 42\n    r = &inner  # error: inner is in a deeper scope than r\n  end           # inner goes out of scope, r would dangle\n\nFix: Ensure borrowed variables live at least as long as their borrows.\nMove the source variable to an outer scope, or restructure to avoid\ncross-scope borrows.\n"

## Explain error code QZ1301: Type is not Send.
def explain_QZ1301(): String = "## QZ1301: Type is Not Send\n\nA value of a type that is not Send was captured by a closure passed to\n'spawn' or 'taskgroup_spawn'. Types that are not Send cannot cross\nthread boundaries because their semantics are undefined in a\nmulti-threaded context.\n\nTypes that are NOT Send: CPtr, Ptr (raw pointers with unknown semantics).\n\nExample:\n\n  var p = cptr_null()\n  spawn(-> ptr_read_byte(p, 0))  # error: CPtr is not Send\n\nFix: Avoid capturing non-Send types in spawned closures. Copy the\nunderlying data into a Send-safe type before spawning, or use a\nsynchronization primitive like Mutex or Channel to communicate.\n"

## Explain error code QZ1302: Type is not Sync.
def explain_QZ1302(): String = "## QZ1302: Type is Not Sync (Informational)\n\nA mutable heap type was captured by a closure passed to 'spawn' or\n'taskgroup_spawn'. Since closure capture in Quartz copies the pointer\n(not the data), both threads would have aliased mutable access to the\nsame heap allocation — a data race.\n\nThe compiler automatically force-moves the captured binding so it cannot\nbe used after the spawn. Any subsequent access produces QZ1212.\n\nTypes that are Send but NOT Sync: Vec, HashMap, Set, StringBuilder, IntMap.\n\nExample:\n\n  var v = vec_new()\n  spawn(-> v.push(42))  # v is force-moved\n  v.push(43)            # error: QZ1212 use of moved value\n\nFix: If you need the value after spawn, clone it before spawning,\nor use a Channel to send results back.\n"

## Explain error code QZ1401: Use of arena-allocated value after invalidation.
def explain_QZ1401(): String = "## QZ1401: Use After Arena Invalidation\n\nA variable that was allocated from an arena (via arena_alloc, pool_alloc,\nor @arena_name syntax) was used after the arena was invalidated by\narena_reset(), arena_destroy(), or pool_destroy().\n\nThis is a use-after-free bug: the arena has reclaimed or freed the memory,\nso the pointer is dangling.\n\nExample:\n\n  a = arena_new()\n  ptr = arena_alloc(a, 64)\n  arena_reset(a)\n  print_int(ptr)  # error: QZ1401\n\nFix: Do not use arena-allocated values after the arena is reset or\ndestroyed. If you need the data, copy it before invalidating the arena.\nAllocations made AFTER a reset are valid.\n"

## Explain error code QZ1402: Arena-allocated value escapes arena scope.
def explain_QZ1402(): String = "## QZ1402: Arena Value Escapes Scope\n\nA value allocated from a scoped arena (arena name do...end) was returned\nfrom the function. The scoped arena is destroyed when the block exits,\nso the returned pointer would be dangling.\n\nExample:\n\n  def alloc_in_arena(): Int\n    arena a do\n      ptr = @a [1, 2, 3]\n      return ptr  # error: QZ1402\n    end\n    return 0\n  end\n\nFix: Copy the data to the heap (@heap) before returning, or restructure\nthe code so arena-allocated values are consumed within the arena block.\n"

## Explain error code QZ0170: yield outside generator function.
def explain_QZ0170(): String = "## QZ0170: Yield Outside Generator Function\n\nThe 'yield' keyword can only be used inside a generator function.\nA generator function must have a return type of 'impl Iterator<T>'.\n\nExample:\n\n  def bad_func(): Int\n    yield 42  # error: QZ0170\n    return 0\n  end\n\nFix: Change the return type to 'impl Iterator<T>':\n\n  def good_func(): impl Iterator<Int>\n    yield 42\n  end\n"

## Explain error code QZ0172: return-with-value in generator function.
def explain_QZ0172(): String = "## QZ0172: Return With Value in Generator Function\n\nGenerator functions (return type 'impl Iterator<T>') cannot use 'return <value>'.\nUse 'yield' to produce values, or bare 'return' to stop the generator.\n\nExample:\n\n  def countdown(n: Int): impl Iterator<Int>\n    var i = n\n    while i > 0\n      yield i\n      i = i - 1\n    end\n    return 42  # error: QZ0172 — use yield instead\n  end\n\nFix: Use 'yield' for values, 'return' (no value) to stop:\n\n  def countdown(n: Int): impl Iterator<Int>\n    var i = n\n    while i > 0\n      yield i\n      i = i - 1\n    end\n    # generator stops naturally at end of function\n  end\n"

## Explain error code QZ0173: async of non-call-expression.
def explain_QZ0173(): String = "## QZ0173: Async of Non-Call Expression\n\nThe 'async' keyword must be followed by a function call.\n\nExample:\n\n  var x = async 42  # error: QZ0173\n\nFix: Use 'async' with a function call:\n\n  var f = async fetch_data(url)\n  var data = await f\n"

